/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Difference between Palliative Care and Hospice Care  All palliative care is not the same as end-of-life or hospice care. While both services aim to facilitate symptom management and relief from suffering, hospice is a very specific type of palliative care and the terms should not be used interchangeably. While hospice care is a type of palliative care, not all palliative care is or will be hospice care.)
/E (Difference between Palliative Care and Hospice Care  All palliative care is not the same as end-of-life or hospice care. While both services aim to facilitate symptom management and relief from suffering, hospice is a very specific type of palliative care and the terms should not be used interchangeably. While hospice care is a type of palliative care, not all palliative care is or will be hospice care.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Hospice care is for patients who are not expected to survive their illness or recover from their condition and are nearing the end of their life. )
/E (Hospice care is for patients who are not expected to survive their illness or recover from their condition and are nearing the end of their life. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Hospice is recommended when a condition or illness gets to the point when treatment can no longer cure or control it or the benefit of treatment is outweighed by suffering or burden of care and the patient or family chooses to stop further interventions. For a person to be eligible for hospice care in either of these situations, a physician must certify the patient has a terminal diagnosis, meaning they are not expected to live longer than six months with the usual course of their illness or condition. )
/E (Hospice is recommended when a condition or illness gets to the point when treatment can no longer cure or control it or the benefit of treatment is outweighed by suffering or burden of care and the patient or family chooses to stop further interventions. For a person to be eligible for hospice care in either of these situations, a physician must certify the patient has a terminal diagnosis, meaning they are not expected to live longer than six months with the usual course of their illness or condition. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
